WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.250
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:08.250
I am Dr. Cesar Marolla, Instructor at Harvard University Division of Continuing Education.

3
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:18.250
I developed the Risk Mechanism Theory Index, RMTI, to turn what we can measure into one score that I can follow over time.

4
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.850
Here I apply it to lung cancer.

5
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:29.550
I start with a published case study: Sabakhtarishvili and colleagues, JTO Clinical and Research Reports, 2025.

6
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:37.950
Among 3,647 screened people, stage four at diagnosis was 13 percent, versus 35 to 44 percent under usual care.

7
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.050
I follow the tumor through four stages.

8
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:48.450
Stage one: a small tumor, confined to the lung, with no lymph node spread.

9
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:53.650
Stage two: a larger tumor, or nearby lymph nodes are involved.

10
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.850
Stage three: spread to central chest lymph nodes or neighboring structures.

11
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:04.050
Stage four: distant metastasis, for example to the brain or bone.

12
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.350
Now I assess the data.

13
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:16.950
I use smoking burden for exposure; the two-year cancer detection rate for likelihood; and screening delivery, which lowers late-stage presentation, for resilience.

14
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.350
This mapping is illustrative.

15
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.250
Then I calculate.

16
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.450
R equals L times E times V times one minus rho.

17
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:33.850
With these illustrative inputs, R is 0.098, band two of the fixed five-band scale.

18
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.650
Expected Annual Loss adds consequences.

19
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.550
I follow the trajectory.

20
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.950
At each review cycle I update vulnerability and resilience.

21
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:51.950
In this synthetic example, as screening improves, R falls from 0.098 to 0.044, band two to band one.

22
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.550
It is an illustration, not a prediction.

23
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:04.850
With RMTI, I quantify risk as one score, assess it on fixed bands, and track vulnerability and resilience across cycles.

24
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.650
Treatment decisions stay with clinicians.

25
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:14.550
RMTI is a research prototype: uncalibrated, with synthetic examples, and not a medical device.

26
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.750
I am testing whether trajectory adds information beyond the current level.

27
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.150
Thank you for watching.
